with  

\[
F_{ij}^{\,z}= -\frac{\partial U_{ww}(r_{ij})}{\partial r_{ij}}\,
\frac{z_i-z_j}{r_{ij}},\qquad 
F_{iC}^{\,z}= -\frac{\partial U_{gw}(r_{iC})}{\partial r_{iC}}\,
\frac{z_i-z_C}{r_{iC}},
\]

\(U_{ww}\) the TIP4P/2005 water–water pair potential, \(U_{gw}\) the Lennard‑Jones (12‑6) water–graphene interaction, \(A\) the instantaneous lateral area of the simulation cell, and \(d\) the fixed separation of the two graphene sheets (so that the volume is \(V=A d\)).  

The first term is the ideal‑gas contribution, the second term is the water–water virial contribution, and the third term is the explicit water–graphene (wall) contribution.  Because the normal dimension \(d\) is fixed, the usual isotropic virial expression is replaced by this anisotropic form with the volume \(V=A d\) and the additional wall term.

\[
\boxed{\text{The two constants are empirical scaling factors that control how the intensity term }P\text{ contributes to the denominator of the weight. The term }(0.0441\,P)^{2}\text{ grows as the square of the intensity, so for strong reflections (large }P\text{) it becomes dominant, greatly increasing the denominator and thus reducing the weight. In other words, 0.0441 down‑weights strong reflections. The term }0.8116\,P\text{ grows only linearly with intensity; it contributes to the denominator for all reflections but is most important when }P\text{ is small or moderate, preventing the weight from becoming excessively large for weak reflections. Together they ensure that weak reflections are weighted mainly by the variance }\sigma^{2}(F_{o}^{2})\text{ (or the linear term), while strong reflections are down‑weighted by the quadratic term, giving a balanced contribution of strong and weak data to the refinement.}}
\]

