\boxed{
\textbf{Natural orbital occupation numbers (NOONs)} are the eigenvalues }n_i\textbf{ of the one‑particle reduced density matrix (1‑RDM) constructed from the broken‑symmetry (BS) DFT wavefunction. They represent the average electron population of each natural orbital (NO). 

\textbf{Obtaining NOONs from a BS‑DFT calculation:} 
\begin{enumerate}
\item Perform an unrestricted BS‑DFT calculation and obtain the spin‑α and spin‑β Kohn–Sham density matrices }P^{\alpha}\text{ and }P^{\beta}\text{ (in the atomic‑orbital basis). 
\item Form the spin‑free (or total) 1‑RDM by averaging (or summing) the two spin components, e.g. 
\[
P = \tfrac{1}{2}\big(P^{\alpha}+P^{\beta}\big) 
\quad\text{(or }P = P^{\alpha}+P^{\beta}\text{ for the total electron density).}
\] 
\item Diagonalise }P\text{ to obtain its eigenvectors (the natural orbitals } \phi_i\text{) and eigenvalues }n_i\text{ (the NOONs). 
\item The NOONs satisfy }0\le n_i\le 2\text{; values }n_i\approx2\text{ or }0\text{ correspond to doubly occupied or empty orbitals, while }n_i\approx1\text{ indicates a singly occupied (radical) orbital.}
\end{enumerate}
For the tetraradical intermediate, four natural orbitals will have NOONs close to 1 (e.g., }n_i\approx0.9\text{–}1.1\text{), reflecting the two metal‑center radicals and the two ligand‑center radicals that give the overall }S=2\text{ state.}
}

