Solve the following problem. Make sure to put the answer (and only answer) inside  oxed{}.

Consider a single flexible polymer in a dilute solution that is modeled as a finitely‑extensible nonlinear elastic (FENE) dumbbell. The end‑to‑end vector R(t) obeys the stochastic differential equation

    dR/dt = κ·R - (1/τ) * R / (1 - R^2/L^2) + sqrt(2 k_B T / ζ) η(t),

with ⟨η_i(t) η_j(t')⟩ = δ_ij δ(t - t').

where

* κ is the velocity‑gradient tensor of a planar simple shear flow, κ = [[0, γ̇, 0], [0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]];
* τ is the longest (Zimm) relaxation time of the polymer;
* L is the contour length (maximum possible extension);
* ζ is the drag coefficient of a bead;
* η(t) is a vector of independent white‑noise processes;
* k_B T is the thermal energy.

Define the dimensionless extension r = R/L and the Weissenberg number Wi = γ̇ τ.

(a) Nondimensionalisation – Rewrite the stochastic equation in terms of r and a dimensionless time \tilde t = t/τ. Show that the resulting Langevin equation can be expressed as

    dr/d\tilde t = Wi * r - r/(1 - r^2) + sqrt(2 ε) ξ(\tilde t),

where ε = k_B T/(ζ L^2 γ̇) and ξ is a scalar white‑noise term (you may argue that, after averaging over orientations, the dynamics of the magnitude decouple from the angular degrees of freedom).

