\text{A critical point of a ternary L‑L system is reached when the mixture becomes marginally}\\[2mm]
\text{stable, i.e. the curvature of the Gibbs‑energy‑of‑mixing surface vanishes in at least one}\\[2mm]
\text{direction.  In practice this means that at the critical composition }{\bf x}_c
\text{ the Hessian matrix}\\[2mm]
\displaystyle 
\mathbf H({\bf x}_c)=\Big[\frac{\partial^2 G^{E}}{\partial x_i\partial x_j}\Big]_{i,j=1}^{3}
\text{ (restricted to the plane }x_1+x_2+x_3=1\text{) is singular.}\\[4mm]
\text{Using two independent variables (e.g. }x_1,x_2\text{ with }x_3=1-x_1-x_2\text{), the reduced}\\[2mm]
\text{Hessian } \mathbf H_{\rm red}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\partial^2 G^{E}/\partial x_1^2 & \partial^2 G^{E}/\partial x_1\partial x_2\\[1mm]
\partial^2 G^{E}/\partial x_2\partial x_1 & \partial^2 G^{E}/\partial x_2^2
\end{pmatrix}_{\bf x_c}
\text{ must satisfy}\\[2mm]
\displaystyle\det\!\big(\mathbf H_{\rm red}\big)=0\qquad\text{(zero eigenvalue)}.\\[4mm]
\text{Together with the equilibrium (first‑derivative) condition}\\[2mm]
\displaystyle\frac{\partial G^{E}}{\partial x_i}\Big|_{\bf x_c}=\lambda\;(i=1,2,3) 
\quad\text{(chemical‑potential equality)},\\[4mm]
\text{and, for a true critical point, the third‑order directional derivative along the}\\[2mm]
\text{zero‑eigenvector }{\bf v}\text{ also vanishes: } 
\displaystyle v_i v_j v_k\frac{\partial^3 G^{E}}{\partial x_i\partial x_j\partial x_k}\Big|_{\bf x_c}=0.\\[4mm]
\text{Thus the critical point is identified by }\nabla G^{E}= \lambda\mathbf 1,\;
\det\mathbf H_{\rm red}=0\text{ (and the third‑order flatness condition).}
\end{array}
}
\]

