The dilute‑phase holdup is unchanged by the solids  

\[
\varepsilon_{d}= \varepsilon_{d}^{0}=C_{1}\,U_{g}^{\;\alpha}\,D^{\;\beta}\; .
\]

The effective liquid viscosity is  

\[
\eta_{\text{eff}}=\eta_{0}\bigl(1+A\phi_{s}\bigr) .
\]

Because the solids reduce the coalescence frequency of the small bubbles by the factor \((1-k\phi_{s})\), the buoyancy‑driven rise constant in the dense‑phase balance is effectively \(K(1-k\phi_{s})\).  
Hence, from the first‑order gas balance for the dense phase  

\[
\varepsilon_{n}(0)=\frac{U_{g}\,\varepsilon_{d}}{K\,(1-k\phi_{s})\,\eta_{\text{eff}}}
   =\frac{U_{g}\,C_{1}\,U_{g}^{\alpha}\,D^{\beta}}
          {K\,\eta_{0}\,(1-k\phi_{s})\,(1+A\phi_{s})}\; .
\]

Axial back‑mixing gives an exponential decay of the dense‑phase holdup:

\[
\varepsilon_{n}(H)=\varepsilon_{n}(0)\,\exp\!\Bigl(-\frac{H}{L}\Bigr)
   =\frac{C_{1}\,U_{g}^{\alpha+1}\,D^{\beta}}
          {K\,\eta_{0}\,(1-k\phi_{s})\,(1+A\phi_{s})}\;
      \exp\!\Bigl(-\frac{H}{L}\Bigr).
\]

Finally, the total gas holdup measured at the outlet is  

\[
\boxed{\;
\varepsilon_{T}(H)=\varepsilon_{d}+\varepsilon_{n}(H)
